{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b VcfEdgeAtScale} deploys a vSphere Supervisor end to end in {\b VCF 9.x}, following the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://blogs.vmware.com/cloud-foundation/2025/07/14/modernizing-your-edge-with-single-node-vsphere-supervisor-in-vmware-cloud-foundation-9-0/"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
single-node supervisor design guidance
}}}
. It uses {\b VCF PowerCLI 9.0 or later} plus {\f1 infrastructure.json} and {\f1 supervisor.json} to drive cluster, network, storage, Supervisor, Argo CD, and Harbor. Use {\b {\f1 Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -Initialize}} to lay out or refresh the configuration directory from the module {\b {\f1 Templates}} store (see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#getting-started"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Getting started
}}}
). Use {\b {\f1 Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -CollectLogs}} to build a support zip of your JSON, {\b Logs}, and {\b ServicesYaml} under {\b {\f1 VcfEdgeatScaleRootDirectory}} (see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#support-bundle--collectlogs"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Support bundle
}}}
).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Customer distribution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Validate first:} Set {\b {\f1 VcfEdgeatScaleRootDirectory}} (or pass {\b {\f1 -InfrastructureJson}} / {\b {\f1 -SupervisorJson}}), then run {\b {\f1 Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -ValidateOnly}} (and {\b {\f1 Invoke-Pester}} on the module tests) in a non-production environment before production rollout.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Support boundary:} External links in this README point to Broadcom or community documentation for {\b VCF}, {\b PowerShell}, and {\b Kubernetes}; your support agreement for this automation is defined by whoever ships the package to you (for example your account team or internal platform team).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Identity:} The module manifest uses a unique {\b GUID} for side-by-side installs; do not duplicate the same GUID across forked packages you intend to publish separately.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Prerequisites\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b VCF 9.x and vCenter:} Running environment with vCenter available.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Hosts:} ESXi installed and cabled; management and required data networks reachable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Provisioning host:} Can reach vCenter, ESXi, and the Supervisor management network.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Datacenter:} Already exists in vCenter; vLCM image is present in the Image Catalog (per {\f1 infrastructure.json}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b PowerShell:} 7.4 or newer ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://github.com/PowerShell/PowerShell"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
PowerShell
}}}
).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b VCF PowerCLI:} {\f1 VCF.PowerCLI} {\b 9.0+} on the provisioning host (enforced at startup and when a new log file is created).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b kubectl:} Installed; see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/tools/"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Kubernetes tools
}}}
.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b VCF CLI:} Installed; see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://techdocs.broadcom.com/us/en/vmware-cis/vcf/vcf-9-0-and-later/9-0/building-your-cloud-applications/getting-started-with-the-tools-for-building-applications/installing-and-using-vcf-cli-v9.html"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Installing and using VCF CLI v9
}}}
.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Functions Performed by this Automation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This automation performs the following:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Deployment (per edge site)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 1.\tx360\tab {\b Cluster creation and host addition}: Creates a new cluster in vCenter and adds the specified host(s) to the cluster. If a host is already managed by vCenter before it is added to the cluster, the module refuses to proceed while that host has {\b powered-on} VMs unless you confirm at a {\b Y/N} prompt (default {\b N}); it logs each running VM\u8217's name, power state, and guest OS, and the vCenter server name.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 2.\tx360\tab {\b Cluster configuration}:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab DRS set to Automatic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab vLCM: image selection from the vCenter Image Catalog (by name from JSON or interactive prompt); cluster compliance check and remediation run before vSAN health checks.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 3.\tx360\tab {\b VDS and networking}:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab vSphere Distributed Switch (VDS) creation with required port groups (management, vMotion, vSAN, vSAN Witness as configured) and VMkernel interfaces. Management (vmk0) remains management-only; vMotion, vSAN, and vSAN Witness use dedicated VMkernels when configured. With {\b four physical NICs} (two VDS instances, {\f1 -sw1} and {\f1 -sw2}), {\b vSAN witness traffic stays on the first VDS} ({\f1 VDS-<edgesite>-sw1}): the dedicated witness VMkernel (vmk3) and its distributed port group are created there; vMotion and vSAN VMkernels remain on the second VDS ({\f1 VDS-<edgesite>-sw2}). With two NICs, all VMkernel port groups use the single VDS.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab Physical NIC connectivity check before VDS creation; active/standby uplink teaming; MTU from config (default 9000 for vMotion/vSAN, 1500 for management and vSAN Witness).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab When {\b vSAN Witness} is a dedicated VMkernel ({\b vmk3}), {\b {\f1 gateway}} in {\b {\f1 networkingVmKernelInterfaces}} is applied on each host with {\b esxcli} (VCF PowerCLI does not configure a VMkernel default gateway on create).\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 4.\tx360\tab {\b Storage}:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab {\b VMFS}: Local VMFS datastore from available disk; storage policy for the edge datastore.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab {\b vSAN OSA or vSAN ESA}: vSAN enabled (OSA or ESA per config); disk auto-claim; vSAN witness traffic (and optional witness host); vSAN performance service and alarm checks. {\b Triggered vCenter alarms} with {\b red} status require you to opt in to continue (default {\b N}), or use {\b {\f1 -AcceptBadCheckResults}} to proceed without prompting (same switch also relaxes strict vSAN health / vLCM gates elsewhere). {\b Yellow} alarms log a warning and do not block. vSAN cluster {\b health} (API) remains gated separately after witness where applicable.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 5.\tx360\tab {\b vSphere Supervisor enablement}: Supervisor enabled on the cluster with VM Operator, VKS, Velero, and Argo CD Operator services.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 6.\tx360\tab {\b Argo CD instance}: Argo CD instance created and configured. The script appends the cluster MoRef to the Argo CD namespace (for example {\f1 argocd} \u8594 ? {\f1 argocd-c462}) so multiple supervisors on one vCenter stay unique. It prints the URL and temporary admin password when done.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 7.\tx360\tab {\b Harbor instance}: Harbor registry created and configured from a templated data file so many edge sites stay consistent and easier to maintain.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 At-scale and targeting\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Multiple edge sites}: Deploys all clusters defined in {\f1 infrastructure.json} sequentially, or a subset via {\f1 -EdgeSite "site1,site2"} in the order specified.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Operational modes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Validate only} ({\f1 -ValidateOnly}): JSON checks (including network segments) and YAML path checks only; no vCenter session and no deployment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Compute only} ({\f1 -ComputeOnly}): Cluster, hosts, storage, VDS, and vLCM through remediation; stops before Supervisor, Argo CD, and Harbor. JSON/YAML validation is limited to compute paths (supervisor.json, Argo/Harbor YAML, and {\f1 common.contextName} are not required). {\b {\f1 Start-VcfEdgeAtScale}} does not run {\b {\f1 Invoke-HarborEnvVarPreflight}} in this mode, so {\b {\f1 harborConfiguration}} may still list {\b {\f1 $env:}} secrets for a later full deploy without defining those variables during compute-only preparation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Cleanup} ({\f1 -CleanUp}): Removes the chosen scope without deploying. Values: {\f1 Supervisor}, {\f1 Compute}, {\f1 All}, {\f1 ArgoCD}, {\f1 Harbor}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab {\b Supervisor:} Disable Supervisor; compute stays.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab {\b Compute:} Remove VDS, storage, and cluster; fails if Supervisor is still enabled.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab {\b All:} Disable Supervisor, then remove compute.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab {\b ArgoCD:} Remove the Argo CD supervisor namespace for that cluster; script waits until it is gone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab {\b Harbor:} Remove the Harbor Supervisor Service from the supervisor; script waits until it is gone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab Typed confirmation is required unless {\f1 -Force} is used with {\f1 common.labenvironment} true (for example {\f1 delete harbor for site1}; use your cleanup scope and edge site name).\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Rollback on failure:} Rollback follows the failure point (compute, Supervisor, Argo CD namespace, or Harbor service). {\f1 -RollbackOnFailure} selects always, never, or prompt (Y/N/Always). For a normal fix-and-retry workflow, choose rollback (Y, A, or {\f1 -RollbackOnFailure $true}) before you change configuration and run again. Use N or {\f1 -RollbackOnFailure $false} only when you deliberately keep a partial deployment open for hands-on debugging; when you are finished, run the matching {\f1 -CleanUp} scope (see {\b Cleanup} above) for that edge site so the next run does not stack on a broken half-state.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Helper functions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b {\f1 Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -Initialize}} (and {\b {\f1 -Initialize -InitializeTemplatesOnly}}): Lay out or refresh the configuration directory from the module {\b {\f1 Templates}} store (see {\b Getting started}). When {\b {\f1 VcfEdgeatScaleRootDirectory}} is set and resolves to a {\b fully initialized} layout (same checks as a completed first run), the run states that the variable was {\b detected}, shows its value, and asks whether to initialize a {\b different} directory instead of re-entering the base path (answer {\b N} to reuse that root). The {\b Initialize summary} reports whether the user-level environment variable was persisted. Output is colorized by section (YAML, Docs, JSON, summary).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b {\f1 Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -CollectLogs}}: Interactive support bundle \u8212- zips {\b {\f1 infrastructure.json}}, {\b {\f1 supervisor.json}} (defaults or custom paths), plus {\b Logs} and {\b ServicesYaml} from the deployment root, to {\b {\f1 Join-Path $HOME 'VcfEdgeatScale-logs-<timestamp>.zip'}} (see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#support-bundle--collectlogs"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Support bundle
}}}
).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Show-InfrastructureJsonConfigurationHelp} / {\b Show-SupervisorJsonConfigurationHelp}: Display configuration reference (Key, Required, Notes) for the JSON files (List, Table, GridView, or Auto by terminal width; optional {\f1 -Filter}). When {\b {\f1 VcfEdgeatScaleRootDirectory}} is set and {\b {\f1 Join-Path $env:VcfEdgeatScaleRootDirectory (Join-Path 'Docs' 'infrastructure-config-help.json')}} (or {\b {\f1 supervisor-config-help.json}}) exists, that copy is used; otherwise the module {\b {\f1 Templates}} files are used.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Installation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Manual Installation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 1.\tx360\tab Download the module files from this repository\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 2.\tx360\tab Extract to your PowerShell modules directory:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab {\b Windows}: {\f1 $env:USERPROFILE\\Documents\\PowerShell\\Modules\\VcfEdgeAtScale\\}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab {\b Linux/macOS}: {\f1 ~/.local/share/powershell/Modules/VcfEdgeAtScale/}\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 3.\tx360\tab Import the module: {\f1 Import-Module VcfEdgeAtScale}\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Getting started\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Follow these steps in order the first time you use the module on a provisioning host.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 1.\tx360\tab {\b Install the module} (see {\b Installation} above) and confirm {\b {\f1 Import-Module VcfEdgeAtScale}} succeeds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 2.\tx360\tab {\b Create or refresh the configuration layout} with an interactive run (default base directory is {\b {\f1 Join-Path $HOME 'VCFEdgeAtScale'}}; you can type another path, including an {\b existing} directory). This creates {\b {\f1 Docs}}, {\b {\f1 Logs}}, and {\b {\f1 ServicesYaml}} if needed, copies Supervisor service YAML and documentation from {\b {\f1 Templates}}, and prompts {\b y/N} (default {\b N}) before overwriting files that already exist. If {\b {\f1 VcfEdgeatScaleRootDirectory}} is already set and resolves to a directory that looks like a {\b completed} initialize ( {\b {\f1 Docs}}, {\b {\f1 Logs}}, {\b {\f1 ServicesYaml}}, root {\b {\f1 infrastructure.json}} and {\b {\f1 supervisor.json}}, and all shipped YAML files under {\b {\f1 ServicesYaml}} ), Initialize reports that it {\b detected} the variable, shows the value, and asks {\b Initialize a different directory instead? (y/N)} \u8212- answer {\b N} to refresh/overwrite in place without re-typing the base path; {\b Y} to choose a new path.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab {\b Full init:} {\f1 Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -Initialize} \u8212- seeds {\b {\f1 infrastructure.json}} and {\b {\f1 supervisor.json}} in the base folder when they are missing; if they already exist, you are asked whether to replace each from the module template (replacing {\b {\f1 infrastructure.json}} sets {\b {\f1 common.supervisorServices.parentDirectory}} to this layout\u8217's {\b {\f1 ServicesYaml}} folder; {\b {\f1 clusters[].harborConfiguration.parentDirectory}} stays as in the template for TLS PEM paths).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab {\b Templates only (existing site):} {\f1 Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -Initialize -InitializeTemplatesOnly} \u8212- refreshes {\b {\f1 ServicesYaml}} and {\b {\f1 Docs}} only; does {\b not} create or replace the root JSON files. Use this after a module upgrade to pull new YAML or help copies without touching your edited JSON.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 3.\tx360\tab {\b {\f1 -Initialize} sets {\f1 VcfEdgeatScaleRootDirectory} for the current session} and persists it via {\b {\f1 [System.Environment]::SetEnvironmentVariable}} (User scope, cross-platform) so new PowerShell sessions inherit it automatically \u8212- no {\f1 $PROFILE} edit needed. If the persist step fails, a fallback manual command is printed. If the variable already points at a {\b folder that does not exist} (stale user or session value), {\b {\f1 -Initialize} clears} it from the session and user environment, then continues with the base-directory prompt. Every {\b {\f1 Start-VcfEdgeAtScale}} run except {\b {\f1 -Version}} needs this variable set; it defaults {\b {\f1 -InfrastructureJson}} / {\b {\f1 -SupervisorJson}} to {\b {\f1 Join-Path $env:VcfEdgeatScaleRootDirectory 'infrastructure.json'}} and {\b {\f1 'supervisor.json'}}, and writes logs under {\b {\f1 Join-Path $env:VcfEdgeatScaleRootDirectory 'Logs'}}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 4.\tx360\tab {\b Edit configuration} in the base directory: {\b {\f1 infrastructure.json}}, {\b {\f1 supervisor.json}}, and the YAML files under {\b {\f1 ServicesYaml}} as needed for your environment (see {\b Configure template files} below).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 5.\tx360\tab {\b Optional:} {\f1 Show-InfrastructureJsonConfigurationHelp} / {\f1 Show-SupervisorJsonConfigurationHelp} for field-level reference.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 6.\tx360\tab {\b Validate:} {\f1 Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -ValidateOnly} (still requires {\b {\f1 VcfEdgeatScaleRootDirectory}} set).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 7.\tx360\tab {\b Deploy:} {\f1 Start-VcfEdgeAtScale} (add {\b {\f1 -EdgeSite}}, {\b {\f1 -ComputeOnly}}, etc. as required).\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b {\f1 -Version}} does not require {\b {\f1 VcfEdgeatScaleRootDirectory}}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Support bundle (-CollectLogs)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Use {\b {\f1 Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -CollectLogs}} when you need a single archive for troubleshooting or support (no vCenter session, no deployment).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Deployment root:} Uses {\b {\f1 VcfEdgeatScaleRootDirectory}} when set; if unset, you are prompted for the folder that contains {\b {\f1 Logs}} and {\b {\f1 ServicesYaml}}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b JSON files:} You are asked whether to use the default {\b {\f1 infrastructure.json}} and {\b {\f1 supervisor.json}} under that root, or to type full paths to other files.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Included in the zip:} Those two JSON files plus the contents of {\b {\f1 Logs}} and {\b {\f1 ServicesYaml}} under the deployment root.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Output file:} {\b {\f1 Join-Path $HOME 'VcfEdgeatScale-logs-<timestamp>.zip'}} (timestamp format {\b {\f1 yyyyMMdd-HHmmss}}).\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Do not combine {\b {\f1 -CollectLogs}} with {\b {\f1 -Initialize}}, {\b {\f1 -InitializeTemplatesOnly}}, or {\b {\f1 -Version}}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Quick Start\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 1. What {\f1 -Initialize} places on disk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After {\b {\f1 Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -Initialize}} (or {\b {\f1 -Initialize -InitializeTemplatesOnly}}), the base directory contains:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b {\f1 infrastructure.json}} / {\b {\f1 supervisor.json}} at the base (full init only, unless you decline replacement when they already exist)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b {\f1 ServicesYaml/}} \u8212- {\f1 1.1.0-25100889.yml}, {\f1 argocd-deployment.yml}, {\f1 harbor-data-values-v2.14.2.yml}, {\f1 legacy-harbor-svs-v2.14.2+vmware.2-vks.1-25220498.yml} (list is {\b {\f1 VcfEdgeAtScaleServiceYamlTemplateFileNames}} in {\b {\f1 VcfEdgeAtScale.psm1}} when filenames change)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b {\f1 Docs/}} \u8212- {\f1 infrastructure-config-help.json}, {\f1 supervisor-config-help.json}, {\f1 README.rtf}, {\f1 EXAMPLE.rtf}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b {\f1 Logs/}} \u8212- daily deployment logs once you run {\b {\f1 Start-VcfEdgeAtScale}} (not created by init alone beyond the empty folder)\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 2. Configure Template Files\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Edit the template files with your environment-specific values:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b infrastructure.json}: Update vCenter details, ESX hosts, cluster name, network settings, storage configuration. {\b Supervisor service YAML} paths are normally {\f1 Join-Path(supervisorServices.parentDirectory, *YamlFileName)} (cluster can override {\f1 parentDirectory} or each file name). If you omit {\f1 parentDirectory} or a matching {\f1 *YamlFileName}, the module falls back to legacy full paths in {\b {\f1 supervisorServices.argoCdOperatorYamlPath}}, {\b {\f1 argoCdDeploymentYamlPath}}, {\b {\f1 harborDataTemplateYamlPath}}, and {\b {\f1 harborServiceYamlPath}} (cluster overrides common per key). {\b Harbor TLS} PEMs use {\f1 Join-Path(harborConfiguration.parentDirectory, fileName)} when {\b {\f1 harborConfiguration.parentDirectory}} is set; if it is omitted, set {\b {\f1 tlsCrt}}, {\b {\f1 tlsKey}}, and {\b {\f1 caCrt}} to full or infrastructure-relative paths. After combining or resolving, paths use the same directory search as the module\u8217's path resolver (current directory, then the infrastructure JSON folder).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b supervisor.json}: Configure supervisor cluster settings, network IP ranges, load balancer configuration\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b argocd-deployment.yml}: Configure ArgoCD instance settings. {\b Note:} The namespace value in this file will be automatically modified during deployment to ensure uniqueness (the cluster MoRef identifier will be appended). The namespace value in {\f1 infrastructure.json} should match the base namespace value in this file.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b 1.1.0-25100889.yml}: Typically no changes needed (ArgoCD operator package)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b harbor-data-values-v2.14.2.yml}: Configure Harbor registry data values for supervisor service deployment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b legacy-harbor-svs-v2.14.2+vmware.2-vks.1-25220498.yml}: Typically no changes needed (Harbor Supervisor Service package)\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Need help configuring your JSON files?} Use the built-in helper functions to view detailed configuration reference tables:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 # View infrastructure.json configuration reference (auto-detects best format)\line
Show-InfrastructureJsonConfigurationHelp\line
\line
# View supervisor.json configuration reference (auto-detects best format)\line
Show-SupervisorJsonConfigurationHelp\line
\line
# Use list format for narrow screens\line
Show-InfrastructureJsonConfigurationHelp -Format List\line
Show-SupervisorJsonConfigurationHelp -Format List\line
\line
# Use table format for wide screens (with separators for readability)\line
Show-InfrastructureJsonConfigurationHelp -Format Table\line
Show-SupervisorJsonConfigurationHelp -Format Table\line
\line
# Use interactive grid view (Windows PowerShell only)\line
Show-InfrastructureJsonConfigurationHelp -Format GridView\line
Show-SupervisorJsonConfigurationHelp -Format GridView\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Format Options:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Auto} (default): Automatically selects the best format based on terminal width. Uses 'List' for narrow screens (< 120 characters) and 'Table' for wide screens (\u8805 ? 120 characters).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b List}: Displays each field on its own line. Works best for narrow screens (40-50+ characters). No column wrapping issues.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Table}: Displays data in a table format with horizontal separators between rows. Best for wide screens (120+ characters).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b GridView}: Opens an interactive grid view window with sorting and filtering capabilities. Works on any screen size but requires Windows PowerShell (not available in PowerShell Core on macOS/Linux).\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These functions display a reference table with {\b Key}, {\b Required} (Yes/No/Conditional), and {\b Notes} for each configuration field.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 3. Run Deployment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Set {\b {\f1 VcfEdgeatScaleRootDirectory}} to your base directory (for example after {\b {\f1 Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -Initialize}}, or manually). Then:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 # Deploy using default infrastructure.json and supervisor.json under the root directory\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale\line
\line
# Validate configuration files only (no vCenter connection or deployment)\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -ValidateOnly\line
\line
# Or specify custom file paths and log level\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale `\line
    -InfrastructureJson "./config/infrastructure.json" `\line
    -SupervisorJson "./config/supervisor.json" `\line
    -LogLevel INFO\line
\line
# Deploy a single site, or run cleanup (Supervisor / Compute / All / ArgoCD), or compute-only\line
# See the Start-VcfEdgeAtScale section below for more examples.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Module Functions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Start-VcfEdgeAtScale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Main deployment function that automates the complete vSphere Supervisor deployment process.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Parameters:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 AcceptBadCheckResults} (Switch, optional) - When specified, automatically proceed when vSAN cluster health is red or when vLCM cluster compliance remediation fails (no Y/N prompts).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 CleanUp} (String, optional) - Cleanup only; no deploy. Must be {\f1 Supervisor}, {\f1 Compute}, {\f1 All}, {\f1 ArgoCD}, or {\f1 Harbor}. {\b Supervisor} disables Supervisor only. {\b Compute} removes VDS, storage, and cluster (fails if Supervisor exists). {\b All} disables Supervisor then removes compute. {\b ArgoCD} removes only the Argo CD supervisor namespace (polls until gone). {\b Harbor} removes only the Harbor Supervisor Service (polls until gone). Confirm with a typed phrase such as {\f1 delete harbor for site1} unless {\f1 -Force} is used with {\f1 common.labenvironment} true (see module help for the exact pattern).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 CollectLogs} (Switch, optional) - Interactive support bundle only: zips default or custom {\b {\f1 infrastructure.json}} / {\b {\f1 supervisor.json}} plus {\b {\f1 Logs}} and {\b {\f1 ServicesYaml}} from the deployment root to {\b {\f1 Join-Path $HOME 'VcfEdgeatScale-logs-<timestamp>.zip'}}. Mutually exclusive with {\b {\f1 -Initialize}}, {\b {\f1 -InitializeTemplatesOnly}}, and {\b {\f1 -Version}}. See {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#support-bundle--collectlogs"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Support bundle ({\f1 -CollectLogs})
}}}
.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 ComputeOnly} (Switch, optional) - Run all pre-supervisor steps (clusters, hosts, storage, VDS, vLCM) then exit without enabling the supervisor or deploying ArgoCD.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 EdgeSite} (String, optional) - Comma-delimited list of edge site names (e.g. {\f1 "site1,site2"}). Deploy only clusters whose {\f1 edgeSite} matches one of the values, in the order listed. Omit to deploy all clusters. Only comma is allowed as separator; invalid delimiters or unknown site names cause the workflow to fail.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 Force} (Switch, optional) - When {\f1 common.labenvironment} is true in infrastructure JSON, bypasses the cleanup confirmation prompt when using {\f1 -CleanUp}. Has no effect otherwise; a warning is shown if used without lab.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 Initialize} (Switch, optional) - Interactive layout: creates {\b {\f1 Docs}}, {\b {\f1 Logs}}, {\b {\f1 ServicesYaml}} when missing; copies module YAML and {\b {\f1 Docs}} files with overwrite prompts; seeds or optionally replaces root {\b {\f1 infrastructure.json}} / {\b {\f1 supervisor.json}}; sets {\b {\f1 $env:VcfEdgeatScaleRootDirectory}} for the current session and persists it via {\b {\f1 [System.Environment]::SetEnvironmentVariable}} (User scope, cross-platform) so new sessions inherit it automatically. If {\b {\f1 VcfEdgeatScaleRootDirectory}} already points at a fully initialized tree, reports detection of the variable and asks whether to use a {\b different} directory instead of the default base path prompt. Other deployment switches are ignored except {\b {\f1 LogLevel}} and {\b {\f1 InitializeTemplatesOnly}}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 InitializeTemplatesOnly} (Switch, optional) - Use with {\b {\f1 -Initialize}}. Refreshes {\b {\f1 ServicesYaml}} and {\b {\f1 Docs}} from {\b {\f1 Templates}} only; does not create or replace root JSON. {\b {\f1 InitializeTemplatesOnly}} without {\b {\f1 -Initialize}} throws.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 InfrastructureJson} (String, optional) - Path to infrastructure configuration JSON file. When omitted (or null/empty), defaults to {\b {\f1 Join-Path $env:VcfEdgeatScaleRootDirectory 'infrastructure.json'}} (requires {\b {\f1 VcfEdgeatScaleRootDirectory}} except {\b {\f1 -Version}}). {\b Inside} the JSON, {\f1 common.supervisorServices} / {\f1 clusters[].supervisorServices} may use {\b {\f1 parentDirectory}} plus {\b {\f1 argoCdOperatorYamlFileName}}, {\b {\f1 argoCdDeploymentYamlFileName}}, {\b {\f1 harborDataTemplateYamlFileName}}, and {\b {\f1 harborServiceYamlFileName}} (cluster overrides common per key), {\b or} legacy {\b {\f1 argoCdOperatorYamlPath}}, {\b {\f1 argoCdDeploymentYamlPath}}, {\b {\f1 harborDataTemplateYamlPath}}, and {\b {\f1 harborServiceYamlPath}} when the directory + file name style is not used. {\b {\f1 harborConfiguration}} may use {\b {\f1 parentDirectory}} with {\b {\f1 tlsCrt}}, {\b {\f1 tlsKey}}, and {\b {\f1 caCrt}} as file names, {\b or} omit {\b {\f1 parentDirectory}} and supply full paths for those PEM fields. Paths are resolved against the current directory and the infrastructure JSON folder before existence checks.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 LogLevel} (String, optional) - Minimum log level for console output. Valid values: {\f1 DEBUG}, {\f1 INFO}, {\f1 ADVISORY}, {\f1 WARNING}, {\f1 EXCEPTION}, {\f1 ERROR}. Default: {\f1 "INFO"}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 RollbackOnFailure} (Boolean, optional) - When {\f1 $true}: always rollback on failure (no prompt; for autonomous runs). When {\f1 $false}: never rollback; leave site in current state and continue to next site if any. When omitted: prompt (Y/N/Always). Rollback scope is automatic by failure state (see {\b Rollback behavior} below). {\b Operational guidance:} answer Y (or use {\f1 $true}) before you re-try after a failure so the site is torn back to a consistent state. Use {\f1 $false} or N only for intentional leave-as-is debugging; afterward run the appropriate {\f1 -CleanUp} for that scope and edge site (for example {\f1 -CleanUp Harbor -EdgeSite site1}) before another full deployment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 SaveHarborYaml} (Switch, optional) - When specified, the per-site Harbor data values YAML generated at runtime is moved to a {\f1 HarborYaml} subdirectory under the module directory instead of being deleted after installation. {\b Warning:} this file contains Harbor passwords and secrets in plain text. A {\f1 [WARNING]} is shown whenever this switch is used. Delete the file immediately after inspecting it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 SupervisorJson} (String, optional) - Path to supervisor configuration JSON file. When omitted (or null/empty), defaults to {\b {\f1 Join-Path $env:VcfEdgeatScaleRootDirectory 'supervisor.json'}}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 ValidateOnly} (Switch, optional) - Run JSON validation (shallow, deeper, network segment uniqueness) and YAML checks where applicable, then exit without connecting to vCenter or deploying. With {\b {\f1 disableArgoCD}} / {\b {\f1 disableHarbor}} on all clusters in scope, Argo- or Harbor-specific rules and YAML checks are skipped; use {\b {\f1 -ValidateOnly -ComputeOnly}} to validate only compute-related JSON. Use to validate configuration files before deployment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 Version} (Switch, optional) - Display module version and exit.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Rollback behavior:} Scope follows where the run failed: compute or vSAN before Supervisor \u8594 ? full compute teardown; after compute but before Argo CD \u8594 ? Supervisor disabled only; Argo CD issues \u8594 ? namespace removed, Supervisor kept; Harbor issues \u8594 ? Harbor service removed only. {\f1 -RollbackOnFailure} sets prompt, always, or never.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Re-trying after a failure:} Prefer rolling back first (interactive Y or A, or {\f1 -RollbackOnFailure $true}). Skipping rollback ({\f1 N} or {\f1 -RollbackOnFailure $false}) is for manual inspection only. After debugging, either re-run and choose rollback at the next failure prompt, or force a clean baseline with {\f1 -CleanUp} (same scopes as {\b Cleanup} above): for example {\f1 Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -CleanUp ArgoCD -EdgeSite site1} if only the Argo CD namespace should be removed, {\f1 -CleanUp Harbor} if only Harbor should be removed, {\f1 -CleanUp Supervisor} to disable the supervisor, {\f1 -CleanUp Compute} to remove compute (fails if the supervisor is still enabled), or {\f1 -CleanUp All} for supervisor then compute. Then fix your JSON or YAML and deploy again.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Examples:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 # Basic deployment with default files\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale\line
\line
# Deployment with custom configuration files\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale `\line
    -InfrastructureJson "config/prod-infrastructure.json" `\line
    -SupervisorJson "config/prod-supervisor.json"\line
\line
# Deployment with DEBUG logging for troubleshooting\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -LogLevel DEBUG\line
\line
# Deploy only a specific edge site (targeted deployments or troubleshooting)\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -EdgeSite "site1"\line
\line
# Deploy multiple edge sites in order (comma-delimited; only comma is allowed as separator)\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -EdgeSite "site1,site2"\line
\line
# Deploy specific edge site(s) with custom configuration files\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale `\line
    -EdgeSite "site2" `\line
    -InfrastructureJson "./config/infrastructure.json" `\line
    -SupervisorJson "./config/supervisor.json"\line
\line
# Autonomous run: always rollback on failure without prompting\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -RollbackOnFailure $true\line
\line
# Never rollback on failure; leave site in current state and continue to next site\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -RollbackOnFailure $false\line
\line
# Compute-only: prepare clusters, hosts, storage, VDS, and vLCM; exit without enabling supervisor\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -ComputeOnly\line
\line
# Cleanup supervisor only for site1 (compute remains); you must type "delete supervisor for site1" to confirm\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -CleanUp Supervisor -EdgeSite "site1"\line
\line
# Cleanup ArgoCD namespace only for site1 (supervisor and compute remain); confirm with "delete argocd for site1"\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -CleanUp ArgoCD -EdgeSite "site1"\line
\line
# Cleanup Harbor Supervisor Service only for site1 (supervisor, ArgoCD, and compute remain); confirm with "delete harbor for site1"\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -CleanUp Harbor -EdgeSite "site1"\line
\line
# Cleanup compute only for site1 (fails if supervisor is deployed); confirm with "delete compute for site1"\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -CleanUp Compute -EdgeSite "site1"\line
\line
# Cleanup all (supervisor then compute) for all sites; confirm per site with "delete all for <edgeSite>"\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -CleanUp All\line
\line
# Cleanup all with -Force when common.labenvironment is true in infrastructure.json (bypasses confirmation)\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -CleanUp All -Force\line
\line
# Accept bad vSAN health or vLCM compliance (proceed without Y/N prompts)\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -AcceptBadCheckResults\line
\line
# Validate JSON and YAML only (no deployment) using files under VcfEdgeatScaleRootDirectory\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -ValidateOnly\line
\line
# Or validate explicit paths (VcfEdgeatScaleRootDirectory must still be set; these paths override the default JSON filenames only)\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -ValidateOnly -InfrastructureJson "./config/infrastructure.json" -SupervisorJson "./config/supervisor.json"\line
\line
# Check module version\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -Version\line
\line
# Support bundle: zip JSON + Logs + ServicesYaml to $HOME (interactive prompts)\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -CollectLogs\line
\line
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\line
# Harbor: pre-set environment variable secrets before running (recommended).\line
# Each $env: reference in harborConfiguration is resolved from these variables.\line
# SECRET_KEY must be exactly 16 characters (AES-128 requirement).\line
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\line
$env:HARBOR_ADMIN_PASSWORD = "MyAdminPassword1"\line
$env:SECRET_KEY             = "MySecretKey12345"   # exactly 16 characters\line
$env:DATABASE_PASSWORD      = "MyDatabasePw1"\line
$env:CORE_SECRET_KEY        = "MyCoreSecret1"\line
$env:JOBSERVICE_SECRET_KEY  = "MyJobSvcSecret1"\line
$env:REGISTRY_SECRET_KEY    = "MyRegSecret1"\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -EdgeSite "site1"\line
\line
# Harbor: omit env vars to use just-in-time interactive prompting.\line
# Any $env: reference that is unset triggers a masked prompt at startup\line
# (before vCenter connection or any deployment step). The value is cached\line
# in the process environment for the rest of the run \u8212- only prompted once.\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -EdgeSite "site1"\line
# Console output when HARBOR_ADMIN_PASSWORD and SECRET_KEY are unset:\line
#   [INFO] harborConfiguration.harborAdminPassword references environment variable\line
#          "HARBOR_ADMIN_PASSWORD" which is not set.\line
#   Enter value for HARBOR_ADMIN_PASSWORD (input masked): ****\line
#   [INFO] harborConfiguration.secretKey references environment variable\line
#          "SECRET_KEY" which is not set.\line
#   Enter value for SECRET_KEY (input masked): ****************\line
\line
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\line
# ArgoCD: roll back the ArgoCD namespace only, fix YAML, and retry.\line
# Supervisor and Harbor remain intact; their steps are idempotently skipped.\line
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -CleanUp ArgoCD -EdgeSite "site1"\line
# ... fix argocd-deployment.yml ...\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -EdgeSite "site1"\line
\line
# ArgoCD: after a successful deployment the console shows the temporary password\line
# and the command to change it:\line
#   [INFO] Login as user "admin" using temporary password: vEHJgoj3wGtHQDlI\line
#   [INFO] To update your password run:\line
#          "argocd.exe account update-password --server 10.40.12.203 --account admin --insecure"\line
\line
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\line
# Harbor: roll back Harbor only, fix configuration, and retry.\line
# Supervisor and ArgoCD remain intact; their steps are idempotently skipped.\line
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -CleanUp Harbor -EdgeSite "site1"\line
# ... fix infrastructure.json harborConfiguration ...\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -EdgeSite "site1"\line
\line
# Autonomous run with Harbor and ArgoCD: always rollback on failure, no prompts\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -RollbackOnFailure $true\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b EdgeSite Parameter Usage:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\f1 -EdgeSite} parameter lets you target one or more edge sites from your infrastructure.json instead of processing all clusters. Use a comma-delimited list to deploy multiple sites (e.g. {\f1 -EdgeSite "site1,site2"}). This is useful for the following:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Targeted deployments}: Deploy or redeploy specific edge sites without processing others\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Troubleshooting}: Isolate issues to specific clusters\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Incremental rollouts}: Deploy a subset of sites in a chosen order\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Testing}: Validate configuration for selected sites before deploying all\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b How it works:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab When {\f1 -EdgeSite} is {\b not specified}: All clusters in the {\f1 clusters[]} array are processed sequentially. The script connects to vCenter once and processes each cluster in order, maintaining the connection between clusters that share the same vCenter FQDN.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab When {\f1 -EdgeSite} {\b is specified}: Only clusters whose {\f1 edgeSite} matches one of the comma-separated values are processed, in the order you list them (e.g. {\f1 -EdgeSite "site2,site1"} deploys site2 then site1).\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Important Notes:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Use {\b only a comma} to separate site names. Invalid delimiters (e.g. semicolon) cause the workflow to fail.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Each value must exactly match an {\f1 edgeSite} in your infrastructure.json {\f1 clusters[]} array. If any specified site is invalid, the workflow fails with the list of valid values.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab All clusters in the same infrastructure.json share the same vCenter connection (from {\f1 common.vCenterName}), so the connection persists when processing multiple clusters.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Show-InfrastructureJsonConfigurationHelp\par}
