{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Execution Steps\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 1. Install VCF.PowerCLI Module\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Install the required PowerCLI module for VCF.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 2. Install module and lay out configuration\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Recommended:} Install {\b VcfEdgeAtScale} per {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#installation"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Installation
}}}
, run {\b {\f1 Import-Module VcfEdgeAtScale}}, then run {\b {\f1 Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -Initialize}} (see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#getting-started"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Getting started
}}}
). That creates {\b {\f1 ServicesYaml}}, {\b {\f1 Docs}}, {\b {\f1 Logs}}, copies module YAML and documentation, seeds or offers to replace root {\b {\f1 infrastructure.json}} / {\b {\f1 supervisor.json}}, and prints how to set {\b {\f1 VcfEdgeatScaleRootDirectory}}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To refresh only shipped YAML and {\b {\f1 Docs}} files on an existing base directory without touching your JSON, use {\b {\f1 Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -Initialize -InitializeTemplatesOnly}}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Alternate:} If you cannot run interactively, create the same folder layout manually and copy files from the module {\b {\f1 Templates}} directory next to {\b {\f1 VcfEdgeAtScale.psm1}}, then set {\b {\f1 VcfEdgeatScaleRootDirectory}} to the folder that contains {\b {\f1 infrastructure.json}} and {\b {\f1 supervisor.json}}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For sample JSON and {\b {\f1 Start-VcfEdgeAtScale}} usage, see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "EXAMPLE.md"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
EXAMPLE.md
}}}
.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 3. Configure infrastructure.json\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Open {\f1 infrastructure.json} and review all the fields, updating as required for your environment. {\b Accuracy here is crucial for successful deployment.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Set {\f1 supervisorServices.parentDirectory} and {\f1 supervisorServices.argoCdOperatorYamlFileName} / {\f1 argoCdDeploymentYamlFileName} to your copies of the operator YAML and {\f1 argocd-deployment.yml} unless Argo CD is disabled ({\f1 supervisorServices.disableArgoCD}), or use legacy {\f1 argoCdOperatorYamlPath} / {\f1 argoCdDeploymentYamlPath} with full paths.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Configure Harbor template paths and {\f1 harborConfiguration} unless Harbor is disabled ({\f1 supervisorServices.disableHarbor}); use {\f1 *YamlFileName} + {\f1 parentDirectory} or legacy {\f1 harborDataTemplateYamlPath} / {\f1 harborServiceYamlPath}.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Argo CD namespace:} The base namespace in {\f1 argocd-deployment.yml} must match {\f1 supervisorServices.nameSpacePrefix} (default {\f1 argocd}). During deployment the script appends the cluster MoRef (e.g. {\f1 argocd} \u8594 ? {\f1 argocd-c462}) so multiple supervisors on one vCenter do not collide. Edit {\f1 argocd-deployment.yml} only when you need a non-default instance spec; see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://techdocs.broadcom.com/us/en/vmware-cis/vcf/vsphere-supervisor-services-and-standalone-components/latest/using-supervisor-services/using-argo-cd-service/argocd-custom-resrouce-reference.html"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Argo CD service documentation
}}}
.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Quick Reference}: Use the built-in helper function to view the configuration reference table:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 # Auto-detects best format based on terminal width\line
Show-InfrastructureJsonConfigurationHelp\line
\line
# Or specify format explicitly\line
Show-InfrastructureJsonConfigurationHelp -Format List    # For narrow screens\line
Show-InfrastructureJsonConfigurationHelp -Format Table     # For wide screens\line
Show-InfrastructureJsonConfigurationHelp -Format GridView # Interactive (Windows only)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Alternatively, refer to the table in {\f1 Admin.Guide.Single.Node.Supervisor.rtf} for detailed configuration guidance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Optional:} Run {\f1 Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -ValidateOnly} to validate JSON and YAML paths without connecting to vCenter. See {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "EXAMPLE.md"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
EXAMPLE.md
}}}
 for sample output.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 4. Configure supervisor.json\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Open {\f1 supervisor.json} and review all the fields, updating as required for your environment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Quick Reference}: Use the built-in helper function to view the configuration reference table:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 # Auto-detects best format based on terminal width\line
Show-SupervisorJsonConfigurationHelp\line
\line
# Or specify format explicitly\line
Show-SupervisorJsonConfigurationHelp -Format List    # For narrow screens\line
Show-SupervisorJsonConfigurationHelp -Format Table     # For wide screens\line
Show-SupervisorJsonConfigurationHelp -Format GridView # Interactive (Windows only)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Alternatively, refer to the table in {\f1 Admin.Guide.Single.Node.Supervisor.rtf} for detailed configuration guidance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 5. Argo CD operator YAML (optional)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If Broadcom publishes a newer Argo CD operator package than the template ({\f1 1.1.0-25100889.yml}), download it from the product documentation and set {\f1 supervisorServices.argoCdOperatorYamlFileName} in {\f1 infrastructure.json} to that file name (under {\f1 parentDirectory}), or set {\f1 supervisorServices.argoCdOperatorYamlPath} to the full path.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 6. Install VCF CLI\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Install the VCF CLI per Broadcom: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://techdocs.broadcom.com/us/en/vmware-cis/vcf/vcf-9-0-and-later/9-0/building-your-cloud-applications/getting-started-with-the-tools-for-building-applications/installing-and-using-vcf-cli-v9.html"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Installing and Using VCF CLI v9
}}}
. First-run terms and CEIP are described there; set those before unattended automation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Windows:} The installer typically provides {\f1 vcf.exe}. The module accepts {\b {\f1 vcf.exe} or {\f1 vcf}} on PATH (it picks the first name that resolves). Rename or adjust only if your deployment standard requires a specific filename.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Linux / macOS:} Use the {\f1 vcf} binary from the same documentation. Install {\b kubectl} from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/tools/"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Kubernetes install tools
}}}
 if you have not already.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Important Notes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Networks:} Expect the four-network edge layout (management, workload, two load balancer segments) and four VLAN IDs in {\f1 infrastructure.json}, matching the design docs linked above.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b CLI tools:} Install {\b kubectl} ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/tools/"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Kubernetes install tools
}}}
) and the {\b VCF CLI} ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://techdocs.broadcom.com/us/en/vmware-cis/vcf/vcf-9-0-and-later/9-0/building-your-cloud-applications/getting-started-with-the-tools-for-building-applications/installing-and-using-vcf-cli-v9.html"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Installing and Using VCF CLI v9
}}}
) before you run the module (see step 6).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Platforms:} Tested on Windows and macOS with PowerShell 7.4+.\sa180\par}
}
